# QC v4 — Oracle Artist Iteration 3 / Burn 4 (2026-11-21)

_Frame-level QC by Emise under the animator skill §8 protocol. Burns: 4 video generations used of
the iteration cap of 6 (3 agenda burns + 1 failure re-burn); 2 held in reserve rather than spent
re-rolling indicted bases. Register breaks: ZERO across Burn 4 — third consecutive clean burn.
Sheets: `qc4/` (bball-v3-sheet, bball-v4-sheet, ex03-v4-strip, ex02-v4-sheet, gate1-compare-sheet,
intro-qc-sheet), pendulum chart at `qc4/pendulum-frame-chart-final.png`._

## ex02-pendulum — spacing clause settled → PASS (the programme's first ladder Pass)

- The clause (frame chart shows spacing clustered at the extremes) resisted three automated
  trackers, all indicted: diff-centroid (window-light contamination), threshold+component
  (component instability), two-template NCC (median peak 0.44, self-lock).
- Settled by a human frame chart: 40 lantern positions transcribed from dense contact sheets
  (every 6 frames). Extremes decay monotonically (R 83→80→79→74→72→67, L 47→51→53→56 percent of
  frame width); near-extreme steps average 8.4 %/6f against 16.4 %/6f at center crossings —
  spacing clusters at the extremes, as drawn animation requires. Lantern at rest from ≈f204.
- Every clause of the exercise's done-definition is now verified (decay by easing, diminishing
  overshoot arcs, spacing at extremes, camera static in all 20 sampled frames). **Verdict: PASS.**

## ex01-bouncing-ball v3 — FAIL

- Key: dedicated single-object still (wood token alone on a bare counter) — PASSED as a key.
- Clip (10.0 s delivered as asked): the token rests ≈0.8 s, then becomes a ROUND BALL and flies a
  long wandering arc across the frame (f024–f102), returns to the counter, then DUPLICATES —
  two and three discs float at different heights (f132–f174) — before settling as one disc.
- No decaying bounce sequence; no contact squash (there are barely contacts). Camera static ✓.
- The v2 indictment (the busy three-prop key betrayed the test) is itself superseded: with a
  clean key, the failure moved to the generator's object permanence.

## ex01-bouncing-ball v4 — FAIL (failure re-burn, permanence language + small hops)

- The explicit permanence prompt ("only ever ONE token… never becomes a ball, never splits")
  changed the failure mode instead of curing it: no airborne bounce at all — the token MORPHS
  in place (tall loaf → dome → ring/donut → oval, f006–f102), then scale-pulses (grow/shrink
  "hops" without leaving the counter) and drifts right, tilting, to f234.
- **Indicted basis: prompt language cannot hold a lone object's shape or volume constant through
  a prompted transformation in a minimal scene; "squash" wording actively invites morphing** (the
  generator cannot distinguish a moment's deformation from a redesign).
- ex01 stands at four attempts (Burn 2 control PARTIAL with a busy multi-object scene; v2, v3,
  v4 FAIL). Iteration 4 changes the INSTRUMENT, not the prompt: the physics rung goes to the
  drawn route (programmatic frames, the route that produced the P4 tiling and the Gate 1
  prepass), where spacing and squash are exact by construction.

## ex03-fern-at-the-ready v4 — FAIL (two-keyframe route, lowering direction)

- Route: start key = a likeness-checked hammer-raised still; end key = the canonical scene
  still; the clip lowers the arm INTO the canonical key. (Building the raised still by image
  editing failed twice, identically — the editor raised the wrong arm and doubled the hammer;
  still-key editing is indicted as a route to a TRUE end key.)
- Frame 0 is NOT the start key: the generator re-staged the scene (new wider workshop, window
  moved, Fern smaller at frame left). The arm-lowering happens; the final frame matches the end
  key's POSE thematically, not its composition. Fern's buns held in all sampled frames.
- **Indicted basis: two supplied stills are not start/end keyframes to this generator unless
  both come from its own scene instance** (Burn 3's probe, whose end still was generator-native,
  held frame 0 exactly). Independent keys get blended into a re-staged scene.

## ex02-keepers-welcome v4 — PARTIAL

- Key designed for the measured zoom (Waverly ≈1/6 frame height in a doorway arch); text-bearing
  props named out entirely — and the key itself carried none.
- What held: NO text or pseudo-writing anywhere (the named-out discipline works when the key
  carries no text surfaces); register painterly throughout; the nod landed (as a bow, eyes
  closed, t2.5–3.5 s).
- What failed: the landing is again a FOUND composition. The reframing mechanism this time is
  the CHARACTER walking to camera (she leaves the doorway, grows past the room's scale, ends in
  a bust close-up at frame left) — plus invented adornment (jeweled headband, necklace, earrings
  the key does not carry).
- **Indicted basis: a landing cannot be designed from the start key alone while the character
  is free to move** — the generator reframes by the cheapest available motion. Landing discipline
  needs the character pinned by the staging, or found landings accepted and QC'd as their own
  shots.

## Gate 1 — Steamboat Willie: setup + prepass (gate NOT passed)

- Reference copy sourced from archive.org at human pace and hash-verified (see PACKETS.md).
- Arc sketch derived from the original's actual shots (58-frame survey read frame by frame +
  79 detected boundaries): `gate-01-steamboat-willie/ARC-SKETCH.md`, coordinate
  22.6.8/7.6.10/7.5.8, cited in every packet.
- Prepass: 60 hand-drawn repro pages (Emise line renderer: jittered contours + hatching, ink on
  cream) assembled at real shot timing into a full-runtime animatic (467.5 s vs the film's
  461.1 s; +1.4% concat rounding, recorded in the packets).
- Frame-compare QC at 12 matched timestamps: the same shots and beats appear at every sampled
  timestamp (a few seconds of boundary drift at some cuts); compositions match at t=4 (titles),
  24 (boat), 88 (scrubbing), 128 (landing), 152 (hoist), and across the concert beats. Weak
  points, recorded: title lettering ghosts; solid-black figures soften (wash + hatch instead of
  solid black); occasional mid-motion pages read blobby.
- **Verdict: the prepass is delivered and fit as the full-render guide. The GATE IS NOT PASSED —
  by the register's rule a gate passes only on prepass + full render with frame-compared QC.
  The full render is Iteration 4 work.**

## MCU flipbook intro draft — PASS as a draft

- Assembled ranch-side: 1280×720, 24 fps, 360 frames, exactly 15.000 s, silent.
  (a) Gate 1 prepass pages flipping with accelerating holds (26 pages, holds 10→2 frames);
  (b) the woven tiling field delivered in parallel in the Phext P4 manner (720 lines, ballpoint
  blue, hand jitter) and stabilizing; (c) the Fern/Waverly beat taken ONLY from frame-QC-passing
  material (ex01-ignition-v2 frames 84–155 — inside the segment whose 180 frames were enumerated
  180/180 on all five identity anchors in Iteration 2): Waverly unrolls the Great Scroll, its
  town lights, Fern's lantern raised beside her; (d) SCROLLTOWN lettering in over the completed
  field in the Emise font.
- QC sheet (12 frames across all four segments) reads clean: every segment lands as designed;
  both characters on-model through the beat. Soft spot: the flipbook's last page (the drawn
  THE END card) is sparse at small size.
- Filed as DRAFT: `series/intro/mcu-flipbook-intro-draft.mp4`, sha256
  08530873c78efdfadfb7e51d8ac7055f8a21f4ca2d252216edbfe6581b7a6d77.
